    "title": "Justin Rose gives motivational speech for all of us at Masters",
    "text": "AUGUSTA, Ga. — Justin Rose has had more than his share of Masters heartbreak, but the Englishman doesn’t regret his green jacket close calls.“If you’re going to be willing to win them, you’ve got to be willing to be on the wrong side of it as well,” Rose said Monday at Augusta National.What followed was a lesson for everyone who wants to get the most out of their talent, as well as a warning for those scared of failure.“The key is showing up,” Rose said. “The key is to try to be as free as you can in those moments. Yeah, you kind of have to hope a little bit along the way that it’s your day. It could have been my day in a couple of major championships (where) I wouldn’t have had to have done anything different really to be the winner. Hopefully with that mindset, keep chipping away, my day might still happen.”The 2013 U.S. Open champion is in the midst of a career renaissance at 45 years old, and he arrives at Augusta National one year removed from losing in a playoff to Rory McIlroy. It was his second playoff Masters loss, after watching Sergio Garcia win in 2017.“The point is you’ve got to put yourself there,” he said. “That’s the hard part. All we can do as players is to focus on our game, focus on our skill set, and make the odds in our favor the best we can.“I’m very aware that I’ve had tough, tough losses here. I also am aware that I enjoy this place. I don’t want to feel that those three second-place finishes need to create a different sort of feeling for me.”Rose shot a stunning final round 66 last year, but his role in the 2026 Masters always will be remembered as a supporting one to McIlory’s long-awaited career grand slam.“I did everything that I could do,” Rose said. “So I can kind of live with that in a way.”",
